IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pidq-dates`
IMPORT `pidq-discloser`

-- Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010 (Qld) -- worked cases, from the seat of the discloser.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (act-2010-038), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act; the
-- facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every judgment of an entity, a manager, the industrial relations
-- commission or a court is given as a fact.
--
-- Dates worked by hand: January 31, February 28 (29 in 2028), March 31, April 30, May 31, June 30, July 31, August 31.
-- 15 January 2026 to 15 July 2026 is 16 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 15 = 181 days.
--
-- A breached or residual DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the regulative rule is asserted
-- through CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED.

-- HYPOTHETICAL: Priya is a procurement officer at a Queensland local council. She believes, on documents she has seen,
-- that her director steered contracts to a relative's company. Tom, a member of the public, cares for his brother in a
-- disability group home and has seen staff leave residents locked in without supervision. Lena works for a GOC.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 12, 13, 19: can I make a public interest disclosure.
-- Priya, a public officer, about corrupt conduct she honestly and reasonably believes is shown: s 13(1)(a)(i).
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
-- Corrupt conduct needs no "substantial": the answer is the same with the substantial input false.
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
-- Neither limb of s 13(3): she neither believes on reasonable grounds nor does the information tend to show it.
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: you have information about it only if you honestly believe on reasonable grounds that the information tends to show it, or the information does tend to show it (ss 12(3), 13(3), 19(3))`
-- The second limb alone (s 13(3)(b), "regardless of whether the person honestly believes").
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
-- Tom, any person, about a substantial and specific danger to a person with a disability: s 12(1)(a).
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE FALSE FALSE `a danger to the health or safety of a person with a disability` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes, as any person, under s 12: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
-- The same, but the danger is not substantial and specific.
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE FALSE FALSE `a danger to the health or safety of a person with a disability` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the danger, misuse or effect must be substantial, and where the section says so specific (ss 12(1), 13(1))`
-- Schedule 2 offence and contravention, any person.
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE FALSE FALSE `an offence against a provision listed in schedule 2 that endangers the environment` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as any person, under s 12: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE FALSE FALSE `a contravention of a condition imposed under a provision listed in schedule 2 that endangers the environment` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as any person, under s 12: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
-- Any person, about a reprisal against someone else: s 12(1)(d).
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE FALSE FALSE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be a reprisal` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as any person, under s 12: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
-- Tom, not a public officer, about corrupt conduct or a general environmental danger: s 13 is for public officers only.
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE FALSE FALSE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: only a public officer may disclose this kind of matter (s 13); anyone may disclose only a danger to a person with a disability, a schedule 2 environmental offence or contravention, or a reprisal (s 12)`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE FALSE FALSE `a danger to the environment` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: only a public officer may disclose this kind of matter (s 13); anyone may disclose only a danger to a person with a disability, a schedule 2 environmental offence or contravention, or a reprisal (s 12)`
-- A public officer: maladministration, public health or safety, environment, each with and without "substantial".
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `maladministration that adversely affects someone's interests` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `maladministration that adversely affects someone's interests` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the danger, misuse or effect must be substantial, and where the section says so specific (ss 12(1), 13(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `a danger to public health or safety` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `a danger to the environment` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the danger, misuse or effect must be substantial, and where the section says so specific (ss 12(1), 13(1))`
-- Misuse of public resources: substantial, then mere policy disagreement, then not substantial.
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `a misuse of public resources` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as a public officer, under s 13: you may disclose it to a proper authority, in the way s 17 allows`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `a misuse of public resources` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: an alleged misuse based on mere disagreement over spending policy is excluded (s 13(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `a misuse of public resources` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the danger, misuse or effect must be substantial, and where the section says so specific (ss 12(1), 13(1))`
-- Lena, a GOC employee: a fellow employee's corrupt conduct (s 19), and someone outside the GOC (no route).
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE TRUE TRUE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as an employee of a GOC or rail government entity, under s 19: you may disclose it to the GOC or rail government entity or to the Crime and Corruption Commission`
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` FALSE TRUE FALSE `someone's conduct that could, if proved, be corrupt conduct` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: only a public officer may disclose this kind of matter (s 13); anyone may disclose only a danger to a person with a disability, a schedule 2 environmental offence or contravention, or a reprisal (s 12)`
-- Something else.
#ASSERT `Can I make a public interest disclosure question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `something else` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: this is not a matter chapter 2 lets you disclose as a public interest disclosure; another law may still protect you (s 10)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 14-16, 19(2), 20: to whom. Arguments: route, recipient, about a judicial officer, the judicial officer is the chief,
-- corrupt or reprisal, connection.
-- Priya to her State MP (s 14).
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `a member of the Legislative Assembly (a State MP)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: a member of the Legislative Assembly is a proper authority for any disclosure under s 12 or 13 (s 14(1))`
-- Priya to the Crime and Corruption Commission, which can investigate corrupt conduct (s 15(1)(a)(ii)).
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the Crime and Corruption Commission` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: this public sector entity is a proper authority because of its connection with the information (s 15(1))`
-- Tom to a department with no connection with the group home.
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `another public sector entity (a department, council, statutory body, the Ombudsman, a court or the like)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a public sector entity is a proper authority only if the information relates to it, its officers, something it can investigate or remedy, or a reprisal for an earlier disclosure, or you honestly believe so (s 15(1))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `another public sector entity (a department, council, statutory body, the Ombudsman, a court or the like)` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: this public sector entity is a proper authority because of its connection with the information (s 15(1))`
-- A journalist first: not yet.
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `a journalist` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not yet: a journalist may receive a disclosure only under s 20, after a disclosure to a proper authority was not investigated, ended without action, or was not answered within 6 months`
-- A GOC is not a public sector entity (s 6(2)(b)); a rail government entity is not covered.
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the GOC I work for` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: a GOC is not a public sector entity (s 6(2)(b)), so it is not a proper authority for a disclosure under s 12 or 13; s 19 is the route for its employees`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the rail government entity I work for` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: whether a rail government entity is a public sector entity turns on the Transport Infrastructure Act 1994, not encoded`
-- Lena under s 19: her GOC, the CCC; not her MP.
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 19 (a GOC or rail government entity employee)` `the GOC I work for` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: under s 19 you may disclose to the GOC or rail government entity or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 19(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 19 (a GOC or rail government entity employee)` `the Crime and Corruption Commission` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: under s 19 you may disclose to the GOC or rail government entity or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 19(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 19 (a GOC or rail government entity employee)` `a member of the Legislative Assembly (a State MP)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 19 names only the GOC or rail government entity and the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 19(2))`
-- About a magistrate: corrupt conduct may go to the Chief Magistrate or the CCC (s 16(2)); not to an MP (s 14(2)).
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: a disclosure about a judicial officer that could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal may be made to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 16(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the Crime and Corruption Commission` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: a disclosure about a judicial officer that could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal may be made to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 16(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `a member of the Legislative Assembly (a State MP)` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a disclosure about a judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal, or, for corrupt conduct or a reprisal, also to the Crime and Corruption Commission (ss 14(2), 15(2), 16(2), (3))`
-- Other conduct of a magistrate (say maladministration): only the Chief Magistrate (s 16(3)); not the CCC, even connected.
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: any other disclosure about a judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal (s 16(3))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the Crime and Corruption Commission` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: a disclosure about a judicial officer may be made only to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal, or, for corrupt conduct or a reprisal, also to the Crime and Corruption Commission (ss 14(2), 15(2), 16(2), (3))`
-- About the Chief Magistrate herself: she cannot receive it (s 16(4)). Corrupt conduct can still go to the CCC; other
-- conduct has nowhere to go (finding 2).
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a chief judicial officer may receive a disclosure only about another judicial officer of the court or tribunal (s 16(4))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the Crime and Corruption Commission` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: a disclosure about a judicial officer that could be corrupt conduct or a reprisal may be made to the chief judicial officer of the relevant court or tribunal or to the Crime and Corruption Commission (s 16(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a chief judicial officer may receive a disclosure only about another judicial officer of the court or tribunal (s 16(4))`
-- A chief judicial officer, for a disclosure not about a judicial officer.
#ASSERT `Is this a proper authority question` `s 12 or s 13` `the chief judicial officer of the judicial officer's court or tribunal` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: a chief judicial officer may receive a disclosure only about another judicial officer of the court or tribunal (s 16(4))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 17: how. Arguments: kind of authority, reasonable procedure, way, department, governing body, officer.
-- No reasonable procedure: any way, including anonymously.
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` FALSE `some other way` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the authority has no reasonable procedure, so you may make it in any way, including anonymously (s 17(1), s 19(4))`
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a member of the Legislative Assembly` FALSE `some other way` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the authority has no reasonable procedure, so you may make it in any way, including anonymously (s 17(1), s 19(4))`
-- The council has a reasonable procedure. Priya may use it, or go to the CEO, her manager or the integrity officer.
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `through the authority's own procedure for public interest disclosures` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: through the authority's procedure (s 17(2), s 19(5))`
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to the entity's chief executive officer` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: despite the authority's procedure, s 17(3) lets you make it to this person in the public sector entity`
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to someone who directly or indirectly supervises or manages me` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: despite the authority's procedure, s 17(3) lets you make it to this person in the public sector entity`
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to an officer whose function is to receive or act on this kind of information` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: despite the authority's procedure, s 17(3) lets you make it to this person in the public sector entity`
-- A member of the council (its governing body): yes.
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to a member of the entity's governing body` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: despite the authority's procedure, s 17(3) lets you make it to this person in the public sector entity`
-- The Minister: only for a department (a council is not one).
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to the Minister responsible for the department` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the authority has a reasonable procedure and you must use it (s 17(2), s 19(5)); this way is not one s 17(3) allows`
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to the Minister responsible for the department` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: despite the authority's procedure, s 17(3) lets you make it to this person in the public sector entity`
-- Tom, not an officer of the department, to someone's manager there: no.
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to someone who directly or indirectly supervises or manages me` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the authority has a reasonable procedure and you must use it (s 17(2), s 19(5)); this way is not one s 17(3) allows`
-- An entity with no governing body.
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a public sector entity` TRUE `to a member of the entity's governing body` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the authority has a reasonable procedure and you must use it (s 17(2), s 19(5)); this way is not one s 17(3) allows`
-- A GOC with a reasonable procedure: s 19(5) has no s 17(3) alternatives, so even its CEO is not enough.
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a GOC or rail government entity, under s 19` TRUE `to the entity's chief executive officer` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the authority has a reasonable procedure and you must use it (s 17(2), s 19(5)); this way is not one s 17(3) allows`
#ASSERT `May I make it this way question` `a GOC or rail government entity, under s 19` TRUE `through the authority's own procedure for public interest disclosures` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: through the authority's procedure (s 17(2), s 19(5))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 30: may the entity decline; the 28-day review.
#ASSERT `May the entity decline to investigate question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: none of the s 30(1) grounds for deciding not to investigate or deal with it is met`
#ASSERT `May the entity decline to investigate question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the entity may decide not to investigate or deal with it, but must give you written reasons, and you may apply to its chief executive for a review within 28 days after receiving them (s 30)`
#ASSERT `May the entity decline to investigate question` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the entity may decide not to investigate or deal with it, but must give you written reasons, and you may apply to its chief executive for a review within 28 days after receiving them (s 30)`
#ASSERT `May the entity decline to investigate question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the entity may decide not to investigate or deal with it, but must give you written reasons, and you may apply to its chief executive for a review within 28 days after receiving them (s 30)`
-- Reasons received 2 March 2026: 28 days after is 30 March 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for a review question` (`the day` 2 3 2026) EQUALS `the day` 30 3 2026
-- Reasons received 31 January 2026: 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for a review question` (`the day` 31 1 2026) EQUALS `the day` 28 2 2026
-- Reasons received 10 February 2028 (a leap year): 19 days to 29 February, then 9 more: 9 March 2028.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for a review question` (`the day` 10 2 2028) EQUALS `the day` 9 3 2028
-- The last day, the day after, and an application dated before the reasons.
#ASSERT `Is my review application in time question` (`the day` 2 3 2026) (`the day` 30 3 2026) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `Is my review application in time question` (`the day` 2 3 2026) (`the day` 31 3 2026) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my review application in time question` (`the day` 2 3 2026) (`the day` 2 3 2026) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `Is my review application in time question` (`the day` 2 3 2026) (`the day` 1 3 2026) EQUALS FALSE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 32: what must I be told. Arguments: how it reached them, action taken, withholding grounds.
#ASSERT `What must I be told question` `I made it to the public sector entity myself (s 15)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: in writing, at least confirmation that it was received and a description of the action proposed or taken (s 32(1)-(3))`
#ASSERT `What must I be told question` `I made it to the public sector entity myself (s 15)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: in writing, at least confirmation that it was received, a description of the action taken, and its results (s 32(1)-(3))`
#ASSERT `What must I be told question` `I made it to the public sector entity myself (s 15)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the entity need not tell you, because giving the information would be likely to adversely affect someone's safety, the investigation of an offence, or necessary confidentiality about an informant (s 32(4))`
#ASSERT `What must I be told question` `another entity or a member of Parliament referred it to the entity (s 31 or s 34)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `either you or the entity that referred it: s 32(1) requires the information to be given to the person who made the disclosure, or the entity that referred it, and does not say which`
#ASSERT `What must I be told question` `I made it to a member of the Legislative Assembly` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a member of the Legislative Assembly has no duty under the Act to tell you anything; the member may refer it to an entity, which then has the s 32 duty (ss 32, 34)`
#ASSERT `What must I be told question` `I made it to a GOC or rail government entity under s 19` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 32 applies to a disclosure made under s 15 or referred under s 31 or 34, not to one made to a GOC or rail government entity under s 19`
#ASSERT `What must I be told question` `I made it to the Crime and Corruption Commission in a complaint of corruption` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 32 adds no duty the Crime and Corruption Commission does not already have under the Crime and Corruption Act 2001, not encoded (s 32(5))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 20: the journalist. Priya disclosed on 15 January 2026; the 6 months end on 15 July 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I be told whether it will be investigated question` (`the day` 15 1 2026) EQUALS `the day` 15 7 2026
-- 31 August: February is shorter, so the period ends on its last day (28 February 2027; 29 February 2028).
#ASSERT `By when must I be told whether it will be investigated question` (`the day` 31 8 2026) EQUALS `the day` 28 2 2027
#ASSERT `By when must I be told whether it will be investigated question` (`the day` 31 8 2027) EQUALS `the day` 29 2 2028
#ASSERT `By when must I be told whether it will be investigated question` (`the day` 29 2 2028) EQUALS `the day` 29 8 2028
-- Arguments: made a PID, same information, what the entity did, made on, told, told on, today.
-- Not told, asked on the last day of the 6 months: not yet. Asked the day after: yes, under (iii).
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE TRUE `nothing I know of` (`the day` 15 1 2026) FALSE (`the day` 15 7 2026) (`the day` 15 7 2026) EQUALS `no: none of the s 20(1)(b) events has happened; if you are not told whether it will be investigated or dealt with, you may once the 6 months end`
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE TRUE `nothing I know of` (`the day` 15 1 2026) FALSE (`the day` 16 7 2026) (`the day` 16 7 2026) EQUALS `yes: you were not told within 6 months after the date of your disclosure whether it would be investigated or dealt with (s 20(1)(b)(iii)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
-- Told on the last day that it is being investigated: no. Told a day late: (iii) is met, even though it is under way.
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE TRUE `is investigating or dealing with it` (`the day` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`the day` 15 7 2026) (`the day` 1 9 2026) EQUALS `no: none of the s 20(1)(b) events has happened; if you are not told whether it will be investigated or dealt with, you may once the 6 months end`
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE TRUE `is investigating or dealing with it` (`the day` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`the day` 16 7 2026) (`the day` 1 9 2026) EQUALS `yes: you were not told within 6 months after the date of your disclosure whether it would be investigated or dealt with (s 20(1)(b)(iii)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
-- Decided not to investigate, a week after the disclosure: yes at once, under (i).
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE TRUE `decided not to investigate or deal with it` (`the day` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`the day` 22 1 2026) (`the day` 23 1 2026) EQUALS `yes: the entity decided not to investigate or deal with your disclosure (s 20(1)(b)(i)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
-- Investigated, no action recommended: (ii). Investigated and action recommended, told in time: no.
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE TRUE `investigated it but did not recommend any action` (`the day` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`the day` 1 2 2026) (`the day` 1 10 2026) EQUALS `yes: the entity investigated your disclosure but did not recommend any action (s 20(1)(b)(ii)); the disclosure to the journalist is itself a public interest disclosure (s 20(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE TRUE `investigated it and recommended action` (`the day` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`the day` 1 2 2026) (`the day` 1 10 2026) EQUALS `no: none of the s 20(1)(b) events has happened; if you are not told whether it will be investigated or dealt with, you may once the 6 months end`
-- No PID made first; different information.
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` FALSE TRUE `decided not to investigate or deal with it` (`the day` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`the day` 22 1 2026) (`the day` 23 1 2026) EQUALS `no: s 20 applies only after you have made a public interest disclosure under chapter 2 (s 20(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I go to a journalist question` TRUE FALSE `decided not to investigate or deal with it` (`the day` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`the day` 22 1 2026) (`the day` 23 1 2026) EQUALS `no: you may give a journalist only substantially the same information as your public interest disclosure (s 20(2))`

-- The s 20 six-month rule, from a disclosure on 15 January 2026 (day 0): the deadline is day 181 (15 July 2026).
`notified on day 181` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 20 six-month rule for a disclosure made on` (`the day` 15 1 2026)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the entity that received or was referred the disclosure` `notify the discloser whether the disclosure is to be investigated or dealt with` 181))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `notified on day 181` EQUALS TRUE
-- Notified on day 182: too late; the route to a journalist is open and still unused, so the rule is not fulfilled.
`notified on day 182` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 20 six-month rule for a disclosure made on` (`the day` 15 1 2026)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the entity that received or was referred the disclosure` `notify the discloser whether the disclosure is to be investigated or dealt with` 182))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `notified on day 182` EQUALS FALSE
-- Notified late on day 182, and Priya then goes to a journalist on day 190: the permission is used.
`late notice then journalist` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 20 six-month rule for a disclosure made on` (`the day` 15 1 2026)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the entity that received or was referred the disclosure` `notify the discloser whether the disclosure is to be investigated or dealt with` 182), (EVENT `the discloser` `disclose substantially the same information to a journalist` 190))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `late notice then journalist` EQUALS TRUE
-- Going to a journalist on day 100, before the 6 months end, does not use the s 20(1)(b)(iii) route.
`journalist too early` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 20 six-month rule for a disclosure made on` (`the day` 15 1 2026)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the discloser` `disclose substantially the same information to a journalist` 100))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `journalist too early` EQUALS FALSE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 36-39: am I protected.
#ASSERT `Am I protected question` TRUE `being sued, prosecuted or disciplined for making the disclosure` EQUALS `yes: you are not subject to any civil, criminal or administrative liability, including disciplinary action, for making it (s 36)`
#ASSERT `Am I protected question` TRUE `breaching a confidentiality law, oath, rule or agreement by making the disclosure` EQUALS `yes: you do not commit a confidentiality offence, or breach a confidentiality oath, rule or agreement, by making it (s 37)`
#ASSERT `Am I protected question` TRUE `being sued for defamation over what I disclosed` EQUALS `yes: you have a defence of absolute privilege for publishing the information disclosed (s 38)`
#ASSERT `Am I protected question` TRUE `being held liable for my own wrongdoing that I disclosed` EQUALS `no: your liability for your own conduct is not affected by your disclosing it (s 39)`
#ASSERT `Am I protected question` FALSE `being sued for defamation over what I disclosed` EQUALS `not covered: the Act's protections attach to a public interest disclosure; another law may protect a disclosure that is not one (s 10)`

-- s 66: false or misleading information. Arguments: meant as PID, false or misleading, intentional, public officer.
#ASSERT `Could I commit the false information offence question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: an indictable offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment, ordinarily heard and decided summarily (ss 66, 69)`
#ASSERT `Could I commit the false information offence question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: an indictable offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment, ordinarily heard and decided summarily (ss 66, 69); as a public officer it is also misconduct for which you may be disciplined or dismissed (s 67)`
-- An honest mistake is not the offence.
#ASSERT `Could I commit the false information offence question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 66 is committed only by intentionally giving information that is false or misleading in a material particular, in a statement made to a proper authority to be acted on as a public interest disclosure, or in inquiries into it`
#ASSERT `Could I commit the false information offence question` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 66 is committed only by intentionally giving information that is false or misleading in a material particular, in a statement made to a proper authority to be acted on as a public interest disclosure, or in inquiries into it`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 40, 45: was it a reprisal. Arguments: what, how far, the part the disclosure played, reasonable management action.
-- Priya is moved off procurement and passed over for an acting role; the disclosure was the main reason.
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about my career, profession, employment, trade or business` `it was done to me` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE EQUALS `yes: causing you detriment on that ground is a reprisal (s 40); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41), and a tort (s 42)`
-- A substantial reason among others is enough (s 40(5)), even if it was dressed as management action (s 45(2)).
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about my career, profession, employment, trade or business` `it was done to me` `it was a substantial reason, among others` TRUE EQUALS `yes: causing you detriment on that ground is a reprisal (s 40); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41), and a tort (s 42)`
-- Only a minor reason: not covered.
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `financial loss` `it was done to me` `it was only a minor reason` FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 40(5) makes a substantial ground enough; the Act does not say whether a ground that was only minor is`
-- A reasonable performance appraisal, nothing to do with the disclosure (s 45).
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about my career, profession, employment, trade or business` `it was done to me` `it played no part` TRUE EQUALS `no: your manager may take reasonable management action where the manager's reasons do not include your disclosure (s 45)`
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `damage to my reputation` `it was done to me` `it played no part` FALSE EQUALS `no: it was not done because, or in the belief that, a public interest disclosure was or would be made, or someone was or would be involved in a proceeding under the Act (s 40(1))`
-- The director asks a colleague to start rumours about Priya; the colleague refuses: an attempt to induce (s 40(2)).
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `damage to my reputation` `someone attempted or conspired to do it, or tried to get someone else to do it` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE EQUALS `yes: attempting or conspiring to cause you detriment, including trying to induce someone else to, is a reprisal (s 40(1), (2)); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41)`
-- Something outside the schedule 4 list.
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `something else that disadvantaged me` `it was done to me` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE EQUALS `not covered: detriment is defined only by an inclusive list (schedule 4); whether this is detriment is for a court`
-- Intimidation, injury, property, each listed.
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `intimidation or harassment` `it was done to me` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE EQUALS `yes: causing you detriment on that ground is a reprisal (s 40); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41), and a tort (s 42)`
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `personal injury or prejudice to my safety` `it was done to me` `it was a substantial reason, among others` FALSE EQUALS `yes: causing you detriment on that ground is a reprisal (s 40); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41), and a tort (s 42)`
#ASSERT `Was it a reprisal question` `damage to or loss of my property` `it was done to me` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE EQUALS `yes: causing you detriment on that ground is a reprisal (s 40); it is an offence, maximum 167 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment (s 41), and a tort (s 42)`

-- ss 42, 44(3)(b): damages. Arguments: Q10's four, suffered detriment, ADA complaint accepted.
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about my career, profession, employment, trade or business` `it was done to me` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: a reprisal is a tort; you may claim damages, including exemplary damages, and any remedy a court may grant for a tort; a trial in the Supreme or District Court is by judge alone; no prosecution is needed; the Workers' Compensation and Rehabilitation Act 2003 does not apply (s 42)`
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about my career, profession, employment, trade or business` `it was done to me` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: your complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal was accepted, so you can not now commence proceedings under s 42 (s 44(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `damage to my reputation` `someone attempted or conspired to do it, or tried to get someone else to do it` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: damages under s 42 go to a person who suffers detriment as a result of a reprisal; an attempt that caused you no detriment gives none`
-- An attempt that did cause harm (Priya was distressed and lost work): damages.
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `intimidation or harassment` `someone attempted or conspired to do it, or tried to get someone else to do it` `it was a substantial reason, among others` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: a reprisal is a tort; you may claim damages, including exemplary damages, and any remedy a court may grant for a tort; a trial in the Supreme or District Court is by judge alone; no prosecution is needed; the Workers' Compensation and Rehabilitation Act 2003 does not apply (s 42)`
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment about my career, profession, employment, trade or business` `it was done to me` `it played no part` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: on these facts what was done was not a reprisal (s 40)`
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `financial loss` `it was done to me` `it played no part` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: on these facts what was done was not a reprisal (s 40)`
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `financial loss` `it was done to me` `it was only a minor reason` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: whether what was done was a reprisal is open on these facts (see the reprisal question)`
#ASSERT `Can I claim damages question` `something else that disadvantaged me` `it was done to me` `it was the only or main reason` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: whether what was done was a reprisal is open on these facts (see the reprisal question)`

-- s 43: whom. Arguments: employee of PSE or GOC, course of employment, reasonable steps proved.
#ASSERT `Whom can I sue question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the person who took the reprisal and the public sector entity or GOC that employs them, jointly and severally: you may sue either or both (s 43(1))`
#ASSERT `Whom can I sue question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the person who took the reprisal; the employing public sector entity or GOC is liable too, but has a defence because it proves it took reasonable steps to prevent the reprisal (s 43(2))`
#ASSERT `Whom can I sue question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the person who took the reprisal (s 42(1))`
#ASSERT `Whom can I sue question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the person who took the reprisal (s 42(1))`

-- s 44: the Anti-Discrimination Act route.
#ASSERT `Can I complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act question` FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal, even without a prosecution (s 44(1), (4)); but once you complain you may not apply for an injunction (s 50), and if the complaint is accepted you can not later sue under s 42 (s 44(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act question` TRUE EQUALS `no: having commenced proceedings in a court under s 42 about the reprisal, you can not now complain about it under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (s 44(3)(a))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 46: appeals. Arguments: public officer or GOC employee, the action, another Act's right.
#ASSERT `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal question` TRUE `the appointment or transfer of me or another public officer to a position` TRUE EQUALS `yes: you may also appeal it, or apply to have it set aside, on the ground that it was a reprisal, whatever grounds the other Act allows, and even if the decision is only a recommendation (s 46(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal question` TRUE `disciplinary action against me` TRUE EQUALS `yes: you may also appeal it, or apply to have it set aside, on the ground that it was a reprisal, whatever grounds the other Act allows, and even if the decision is only a recommendation (s 46(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal question` TRUE `unfair treatment of me` FALSE EQUALS `no: s 46 gives no appeal on these facts; it adds the reprisal ground only to a right another Act already gives a public officer or GOC employee to appeal or seek review of disciplinary action, an appointment or transfer, or unfair treatment`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal question` TRUE `something else` TRUE EQUALS `no: s 46 gives no appeal on these facts; it adds the reprisal ground only to a right another Act already gives a public officer or GOC employee to appeal or seek review of disciplinary action, an appointment or transfer, or unfair treatment`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal on the ground it was a reprisal question` FALSE `disciplinary action against me` TRUE EQUALS `no: s 46 gives no appeal on these facts; it adds the reprisal ground only to a right another Act already gives a public officer or GOC employee to appeal or seek review of disciplinary action, an appointment or transfer, or unfair treatment`

-- s 47: relocation. Arguments: public service employee, likely reprisal, only practical way, commission considers
-- established, to another department, that CE agrees. (Priya works for a council, not the public service.)
#ASSERT `Can I be relocated question` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 47 gives the right to apply for relocation to a public service employee; other employees' rights are not encoded`
#ASSERT `Can I be relocated question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the industrial relations commission may direct that you be relocated within your department (s 47(5))`
#ASSERT `Can I be relocated question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the industrial relations commission may direct that you be relocated to the other department, whose chief executive agrees (s 47(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Can I be relocated question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the commission can not direct that you be relocated to another department without that department's chief executive's agreement (s 47(6))`
#ASSERT `Can I be relocated question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the commission may direct relocation only if it considers the ground established (s 47(5))`
#ASSERT `Can I be relocated question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the application must be made on the ground that a reprisal is likely if you stay where you work and relocation is the only practical way to remove or substantially remove that danger (s 47(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I be relocated question` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the application must be made on the ground that a reprisal is likely if you stay where you work and relocation is the only practical way to remove or substantially remove that danger (s 47(2))`

-- ss 48-50: injunctions. Arguments: employee, breach of industrial law, ADA complaint made.
#ASSERT `Where can I apply for an injunction question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the industrial commission (s 48); a union entitled to represent you may apply with your consent, and so may the Crime and Corruption Commission if you are a public officer and it may investigate the reprisal; no other court or tribunal but the Industrial Court has jurisdiction (s 48(2), (4))`
#ASSERT `Where can I apply for an injunction question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the Supreme Court, because you can not apply to the industrial commission (s 49); the Crime and Corruption Commission may apply with your consent if you are a public officer and it may investigate the reprisal (s 49(2)(b))`
-- Tom, not an employee: the Supreme Court.
#ASSERT `Where can I apply for an injunction question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the Supreme Court, because you can not apply to the industrial commission (s 49); the Crime and Corruption Commission may apply with your consent if you are a public officer and it may investigate the reprisal (s 49(2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Where can I apply for an injunction question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `nowhere under this Act: having made a complaint under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 about the reprisal, you may not apply for an injunction under s 48 or s 49 (s 50)`

